            <ArticleTitle>Mycobacterium gordonae Skin Infection in an Immunocompetent Child.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/INF.0000000000001549</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Mycobacterium gordonae is a slow growing, pigmented, non-tuberculous mycobacterium (NTM). It is commonly associated with environmental contamination of clinical specimens but it is also a recognized pathogen in immunocompromised hosts. We describe an immunocompetent child with a spontaneously occurring skin ulcer on the face caused by M. gordonae infection.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
